`TcpStream::try_io` does not handle multiple interests

**Version**
tokio v1.22.0

**Platform**
Linux 6.0.8-arch1-1

**Description**

I noticed that `TcpStream` and other types from `tokio::net` with a `try_io` method do not work properly with multiple interests.
Consider the following code:

```rust
use std::error::Error;
use tokio::io::{AsyncWriteExt, Interest};
use tokio::net::{TcpListener, TcpStream};

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn Error>> {
    let listener = TcpListener::bind("127.0.0.1:8080").await?;

    tokio::spawn(async move {
        let mut stream = TcpStream::connect("127.0.0.1:8080").await?;
        stream.write_all(b"some data from the client").await
    });

    let (stream, _) = listener.accept().await?;
    loop {
        let ev = stream
            .ready(Interest::READABLE | Interest::WRITABLE)
            .await?;
        assert!(ev.is_readable() || ev.is_writable());

        let r = stream.try_io(Interest::READABLE | Interest::WRITABLE, || {
            println!("brrr performing io manually");
            Ok(())
        });
        if r.is_ok() {
            break;
        }
    }

    Ok(())
}
```

I expected this code to eventually call my closure and exit. Instead, this program runs forever.
If I replace `Interest::READABLE | Interest::WRITABLE` with `Interest::READABLE` the code works as expected.

[`try_io` docs](https://docs.rs/tokio/latest/tokio/net/struct.TcpStream.html#method.try_io) do not warn about this behavior. If this is intended, I would expect an appropriate mention in the documentation.

I noticed this issue while trying to fix #5098. In my opinion, both issues are related to the `tokio::io::Interest` → `tokio::io::Ready` conversion. If this behavior is indeed unintended, I can submit a PR fixing both issues.

